github_url: "https://github.com/unbraind/pm-github/issues/296"
body: "## Problem\n\nSince #230, a `gh:<owner>/<repo>#N` tag on a local item is only a reference. The importer no longer treats it as identity, which is correct: it no longer overwrites curated items. But importing issue #N now creates a second item that is **not linked** to the local items that already reference #N. Agents and reviewers see two unrelated records for one GitHub issue, often with conflicting statuses.\n\nSourcery flags this on open sync PRs:\n\n- pm-lint#109: \"GitHub issue appears twice in tracker\"\n- pm-rust#194: \"GitHub issues appear twice\"\n- pm-glossary#47: \"Linked issue appears twice\"\n- pm-changelog#383: \"Issues appear twice in release notes\"\n- pm-brief#224: \"Duplicate tracker records remain open\"\n\n## Expected (context management, no identity change)\n\n- During import, find local items that carry the reference tag for the issue being imported, or whose `github_url` field names it. Exclude the importer-owned item.\n- Record a typed relationship between the imported item and each such item, for example `related` or `implements` via the SDK's dependency/link API. Do not merge, retitle or overwrite them. Then `pm context`, `pm get` and pm-changelog can show \"tracked by local item X\".\n- The sync PR body lists these links in a short \"Local items referencing imported issues\" section, so a reviewer sees at once that the second record is intentional.\n- pm-changelog: when an imported issue item and a linked local delivery item close in the same release, they render as one entry. This needs a follow-up issue there; file it if the SDK link is in place.\n- The step is idempotent: a repeat sync adds no duplicate links and leaves the tracker bytes unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A fixture with one reference-tagged local chore and one GitHub issue: the import creates the mirror item with a link to the chore, and neither item's curated fields change.\n- A repeat import writes nothing.\n- With two local items referencing the same issue, both are linked and neither the sync nor the closeout refuses.\n- Every new behaviour has a test that fails on revert."
